Here's a simple example of a Metal compute kernel for Gaussian blurring an image:

```metal
#include <metal_stdlib>
using namespace metal;

kernel void gaussian_blur(
    texture2d<float, access::read> input [[texture(0)]],
    texture2d<float, access::write> output [[texture(1)]],
    uint2 gid [[thread_position_in_grid]]
) {
    float4 color = float4(0);

    // Define the Gaussian weights for each direction (horizontal and vertical)
    const float horizontal_weights[5] = { 0.2736, 0.4598, 0.2373, 0.0288, 0.0005 };
    const float vertical_weights[5] = { 0.2736, 0.4598, 0.2373, 0.0288, 0.0005 };

    // Calculate the sum of weights
    float weight_sum = 0;
    for (int i = -2; i <= 2; ++i) {
        weight_sum += horizontal_weights[i + 2];
    }

    // Apply the Gaussian filter horizontally
    for (int i = -2; i <= 2; ++i) {
        int x_offset = i;
        if (x_offset >= 0 && x_offset < 5) {
            color += input.read(gid.xy + int2(x_offset, 0)) * horizontal_weights[x_offset + 2];
        }
    }

    // Normalize the color by dividing it with the weight sum
    color /= weight_sum;

    // Apply the Gaussian filter vertically
    weight_sum = 0;
    for (int j = -2; j <= 2; ++j) {
        weight_sum += vertical_weights[j + 2];
    }

    for (int j = -2; j <= 2; ++j) {
        int y_offset = j;
        if (y_offset >= 0 && y_offset < 5) {
            color += input.read(gid.xy + int2(0, y_offset)) * vertical_weights[y_offset + 2];
        }
    }

    // Normalize the color again
    color /= weight_sum;

    // Write the blurred pixel to the output texture
    output.write(color, gid);
}
```

This code defines a `gaussian_blur` function that takes two textures as arguments: one for reading the input image (`input`) and another for writing the output image (`output`). The function applies a separable Gaussian filter to the input image using a predefined set of weights.

Note that this is just a basic implementation and may not be optimized for performance or accuracy. You might want to consider using more advanced techniques like separable filters, multi-pass filtering, or adaptive filtering depending on your specific requirements.

Also, keep in mind that you'll need to create a corresponding Metal shader program and pass the necessary parameters to the kernel when executing it from your application code.

