- `@odata.entity`: This annotation is used to mark an entity as an OData entity. It is part of the Open Data Protocol (OData) specification and allows you to expose your entities as RESTful services. When you use this annotation, the generated code will include the necessary metadata to support OData queries and operations.

Example:
```sql
entity MyEntity {
  key id : Integer;
  name : String(50);

  @odata.entity
}
```

- `@sap:entity`: This annotation is specific to SAP and is used to mark an entity as a SAP entity. It provides additional features and capabilities specific to SAP systems, such as integration with SAP Fiori applications and SAP Gateway services.

Example:
```sql
entity MyEntity {
  key id : Integer;
  name : String(50);

  @sap:entity
}
```

You can use both `@odata.entity` and `@sap:entity` together in the same entity definition. However, there is no explicit order of precedence specified in the documentation. Generally, it's recommended to use `@odata.entity` first since it is part of the standard OData specification and ensures compatibility with external systems.

If you're only exposing your entities through SAP-specific technologies like SAP Fiori or SAP Gateway, you might not need to use `@odata.entity`. Similarly, if you're working exclusively within the SAP ecosystem and don't require OData compatibility, you could omit `@odata.entity`.

Here's an example where both annotations are used:
```sql
entity MyEntity {
