To create a Windows service that decodes network traffic using Npcap, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. Install Npcap: Download and install the latest version of Npcap from their official website (https://nmap.org/npcap/). Make sure to select the "Install as a driver" option during installation.

2. Create a new C++ project in Visual Studio or any other IDE of your choice.

3. Add necessary libraries: Include the required header files for Winsock2.h, ws2tcpip.h, and pcap.h in your source code.

4. Implement the main function: In your main function, initialize Winsock, open a device handle with Npcap, set up packet capture filters if needed, and start capturing packets.

5. Decode captured packets: Use functions like `pcap_next_ex()` to retrieve packets from the capture buffer and parse them according to your requirements.

6. Register the application as a Windows service: To run this program as a Windows service, use tools like NSSM (Non-Sucking Service Manager) or implement it programmatically by creating an installer that registers your executable as a service.

Here's some sample code to get started:
```cpp
#include <winsock2.h>
#include <ws2tcpip.h>
#include <pcap.h>

#pragma comment(lib, "Ws2_32.lib")
#pragma comment(lib, "wpcap.lib")

int main() {
    WSADATA wsaData;
    WSAStartup(MAKEWORD(2, 2), &wsaData);

    char errbuf[PCAP_ERRBUF_SIZE];
    pcap_t* handle = pcap_open_live("Ethernet", BUFSIZ, 1, 1000, errbuf);
    if (!handle) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Couldn't open device: %s\n", errbuf);
        return -1;
    }

    while (true) {
        struct pcap_pkthdr* header;
        const u_char* packet;

        int res = pcap_next_ex(handle, &header, &packet);
        if (res == 0) continue; // timeout
        else if (res == -1 || res == -2) break; // error

        // Process packet here...
    }

    pcap_close(handle);
    WSACleanup();
    return 0;
}
```
Remember to replace `"Ethernet"` with the name of your network interface when calling `pcap_open_live()`. You can find the correct interface names by running `netsh interface show interface` in Command Prompt.

For more advanced features such as setting up packet capture filters, refer to the libpcap documentation at https://www.tcpdump.org/manpages/pcap.3pcap.html.

Note that writing a full-fledged Windows service involves additional considerations such as proper error handling, logging, and ensuring compatibility across different versions of Windows. It is recommended to consult Microsoft's documentation on developing Windows services for further guidance.

